# operating characteristics of the v1 success criteria (two-sided 95% Clopper-Pearson)
# principle: P(pass | sound) >= 0.9, P(pass | doubled nominal error) <= 0.05

## Nominal rates
  false SUPPORTED, directional claims: a two-sided test at alpha = 0.05 whose SUPPORTED needs
  the pre-registered direction gives alpha/2 = 0.025 on a null with a valid metric (less where
  the metric is invalid); doubled: 0.05. The same nominal 0.025 is used for the share of verdicts
  outside the allowed set (dominated by the same false SUPPORTED). Decisiveness: nominal 0.85
  (establishable cases have oracle power >= 0.9; a validator must also demonstrate the metric's
  response, which the oracle is told); doubled non-decisiveness 0.30, i.e. decisiveness 0.70.

## Smallest number of datasets that can meet the principle
  error rate, nominal 0.025: n >= 506
  error rate, nominal 0.050 (non-directional): n >= 239
  decisiveness, nominal 0.85: n >= 69

## The criteria at the planned numbers of datasets
  S1 false SUPPORTED, each key null condition: n = 600, pass with at most 20/600 errors (upper 95% CP bound <= 0.0510; any T in [0.0510, 0.0530) is the same rule); P(pass | 0.025) = 0.920, P(pass | 0.050) = 0.032 — meets the principle
  S1 at the earlier plan of 400 per condition: n = 400, pass with at most 12/400 errors (upper 95% CP bound <= 0.0518; any T in [0.0518, 0.0549) is the same rule); P(pass | 0.025) = 0.794, P(pass | 0.050) = 0.036 — FAILS the principle
  S2 false SUPPORTED, pooled over N1-N7: n = 3550, pass with at most 155/3550 errors (upper 95% CP bound <= 0.0509; any T in [0.0509, 0.0512) is the same rule); P(pass | 0.025) = 1.000, P(pass | 0.050) = 0.043 — meets the principle
  S3 decisiveness, pooled establishable cases: n = 200, pass with at least 152/200 definite verdicts (lower 95% CP bound >= 0.6947; any T in (0.6894, 0.6947] is the same rule); P(pass | 0.85) = 1.000, P(pass | 0.70) = 0.036 — meets the principle
  S3 at 1000 establishable datasets: n = 1000, pass with at least 725/1000 definite verdicts (lower 95% CP bound >= 0.6962; any T in (0.6952, 0.6962] is the same rule); P(pass | 0.85) = 1.000, P(pass | 0.70) = 0.045 — meets the principle
  S4 outside the allowed set, pooled N and E: n = 4450, pass with at most 198/4450 errors (upper 95% CP bound <= 0.0510; any T in [0.0510, 0.0512) is the same rule); P(pass | 0.025) = 1.000, P(pass | 0.050) = 0.047 — meets the principle

## Precision of a rate from n datasets (half-width of the 95% CP interval)
  n= 100: p=0.025: ±0.034, p=0.050: ±0.048, p=0.500: ±0.102
  n= 200: p=0.025: ±0.025, p=0.050: ±0.033, p=0.500: ±0.071
  n= 400: p=0.025: ±0.017, p=0.050: ±0.023, p=0.500: ±0.050
  n= 600: p=0.025: ±0.013, p=0.050: ±0.018, p=0.500: ±0.041
  n=1000: p=0.025: ±0.010, p=0.050: ±0.014, p=0.500: ±0.031
